#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124255 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "efccacfd-9595-5315-86c7-de9fefd8ed1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5497" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" :question/worked-solution "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/id #uuid "d7619d9b-e5d7-55e5-96a8-0d05b3532620" :question/math-academy-id "e-5498" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n\n- $B +4I$ is singular\n\n- $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/assets/6273b311b461cc16f516a4d9da6d928643e988c7847cfd14e23401a265d9e25b.png)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124335 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "107369d4-980d-5d73-85ea-8fcf522b96f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8606" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{ \\mathbf{0}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/assets/fd48d2627c161d3e82529aeeb42bcd830c2fc83a9c8d0b7522d1857f099d6f38.png)\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f1ffa3e-f8a4-55f3-aeac-0795ae9f9ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371507} {:answer/id #uuid "84509c98-f383-5fef-80d4-fa06513e4d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371508} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc37c78-13b5-509e-bb2e-25c08f2d2448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371509} {:answer/id #uuid "708bfd5c-b140-52ff-8eb3-1001463e8221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371510} {:answer/id #uuid "87281ea9-47aa-5b26-bb1b-750bda068338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87281ea9-47aa-5b26-bb1b-750bda068338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371511} :answer-field/id #uuid "23355173-e3d6-5c2e-8233-b0a03f1524cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13dbff1b-f209-56fa-97e5-9685250b7352" :question/math-academy-id "q-140834" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10da9e7c-1031-5cd5-8801-76686136c21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371514} {:answer/id #uuid "cf45d593-721d-5356-8539-e3ab768825a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371515} {:answer/id #uuid "e860b64c-4115-5382-b6f4-fbac71373426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371516} {:answer/id #uuid "76d17160-0c6b-5da1-a2de-b73dd6ec1621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371517} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbfad30-cdfe-555f-b731-dcb3e8f34ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf45d593-721d-5356-8539-e3ab768825a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371515} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a03c44e-3c67-5edf-9ecf-39556c89b126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f116f8b-9ad9-560d-9353-a5ae00fb693d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140835" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8d594c9-4229-5730-9c8f-fbb4a53c61ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371521} {:answer/id #uuid "a08f0cc8-15b8-52c4-ba43-fd8807e9f29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371522} {:answer/id #uuid "3616b771-3d73-5a91-ad7d-8b3833d5cd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371523} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f35db3-fdb7-5a72-aad1-b01f8bd1241f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371524} {:answer/id #uuid "d59ba3fc-10bd-5b05-b8bc-10340c27e6b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3616b771-3d73-5a91-ad7d-8b3833d5cd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371523} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b8ee5ed-b197-594c-b314-3788470536d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bb5ae07-a069-50ef-9b09-b0f0d7cf848c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140838" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "671cb847-18d3-5028-8edd-f417c849140d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371528} {:answer/id #uuid "6149ad9c-1c6b-5a3a-993f-8b773c2245ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371529} {:answer/id #uuid "22001f47-1cf7-569b-b2da-6fd85e647b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371530} {:answer/id #uuid "c9059512-fd50-5cd9-9960-f69d25afd6e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371531} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cfebe0-2c3f-53e0-84a7-d2f07d98aed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6149ad9c-1c6b-5a3a-993f-8b773c2245ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371529} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c0bdad-dde9-55f1-b97d-ec4fb378691e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f74faec-319d-5cbc-888b-04c6dad57ef4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140840" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-7I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A-7I$ is singular\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=0$\n- $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-7I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-7I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-7I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-7I$ is singular, then $(A-7I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "922f6dc5-18a2-5f92-b809-544a4bb67f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122975} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7d4e4f-bcdd-5f67-9e2f-6c22c3b52fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122976} {:answer/id #uuid "270749bc-e123-5a86-b43e-8245abff7179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122977} {:answer/id #uuid "6050c1a6-7071-5021-8e89-f4014806d3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122978} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2fbc0f-5bb6-5c0c-a8c7-e22488ed7934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "270749bc-e123-5a86-b43e-8245abff7179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122977} :answer-field/id #uuid "956bd7bf-0b11-54e4-a9dd-d3d307c70f5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3be4d323-0e25-52c5-8eb8-5afe165a1c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-37523" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =3$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-6(3) & =0 \\\\ 27-9-18 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0698cac2-a4c0-5506-85f7-698a05b12465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371535} {:answer/id #uuid "d64553e5-6b51-547d-8757-29ef471e62d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371536} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2734c8-4d9a-58fa-9a27-01ace5ab128a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371537} {:answer/id #uuid "09075b65-d609-5124-8272-3d0ff13dc597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371538} {:answer/id #uuid "d16b5d84-30c2-54ec-95bb-9080a0ac682d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d16b5d84-30c2-54ec-95bb-9080a0ac682d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371539} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0aaaa32-1e93-52d5-8e83-de8cedd7b788" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b867971-ef08-599e-990f-a9ba7a3808c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-37527" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n\n- ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$\n- $(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$\n- ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda =0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could  be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8696fd44-bd4e-5461-b7e5-7287f85bf436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123509} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8c89a1-50a1-5165-96cc-15f61d335b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123510} {:answer/id #uuid "0bedd711-a24a-5644-87f6-6015ccf4c7ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123511} {:answer/id #uuid "381a7f06-4af8-58d7-8b3d-44b4484df291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123512} {:answer/id #uuid "63a7ed2b-095f-5b83-810c-92613569f55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b8c89a1-50a1-5165-96cc-15f61d335b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123510} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb36562-4d5d-5974-b8ea-ba2319a92e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0e0c0ef-5198-55d8-9aeb-10b338580777" :question/math-academy-id "q-47568" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A-3I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-3I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-3I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A-3I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-3I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "badfc21b-e29a-5526-b0b5-50358b9ac921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371542} {:answer/id #uuid "e42da22b-7b40-56f4-a64e-b99e17dc0190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371543} {:answer/id #uuid "9363dd1c-50d7-552a-a86e-7edc94a377aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371544} {:answer/id #uuid "a269ca82-a651-54f6-a96b-03bb1da331a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371545} {:answer/id #uuid "cccdc5df-c635-5ac9-98b7-9788da9358fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e42da22b-7b40-56f4-a64e-b99e17dc0190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371543} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db65f55-a0c3-5cde-8180-6645366cb421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d15ed01c-3403-59b8-954c-3578f1db0619" :question/math-academy-id "q-97871" :question/problem "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n- $B$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =1$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 1-2+1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (2)^{4}-2(2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 16-8+1 & =0 \\\\ 9 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15170bb3-5429-580e-9f87-eb2f476892e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371549} {:answer/id #uuid "33d87a27-cb72-5136-8ac6-7356b6a20cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371550} {:answer/id #uuid "d1296eb2-ff8d-5ad3-8759-6fbea0b2472e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371551} {:answer/id #uuid "31374513-1431-5cd1-a03e-abd5fac38cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371552} {:answer/id #uuid "300d644c-6285-5e3d-9c06-ed1970f5da23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1296eb2-ff8d-5ad3-8759-6fbea0b2472e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371551} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c8ebe06-7444-512a-85c0-1946ddb44573" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ed57511-ac9c-5618-9c74-6969d25be105" :question/math-academy-id "q-97945" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A+I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{rank}(A+I)=3$\n- $\\text{Null}(A+I)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A+I)\\ne 3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beb9c221-dc54-5d62-8b0d-9d521d4f2496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371556} {:answer/id #uuid "59b92376-1fde-5d62-bb46-a9554616eae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371557} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5cfda6-d3b9-5e99-b4f5-e9646fe5bacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371558} {:answer/id #uuid "9433b56a-f4a3-5b64-88e7-c9af182eb8ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371559} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4b3d5e-e51e-5e00-b0b1-c9ec2034407d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59b92376-1fde-5d62-bb46-a9554616eae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371557} :answer-field/id #uuid "073ce2af-1166-5fd8-bac9-65622b7e2a6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38dc057b-5bf1-582d-9d76-8a03bfacda23" :question/math-academy-id "q-97946" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(B-4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{rank}(B-4I)=4$\n- $\\text{nullity}(B-4I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B-4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=B-4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(B-4I)\\ne 4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=B-4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5c24e44-0827-5cfb-8053-1828e76e1c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371563} {:answer/id #uuid "ae79dc86-a664-590c-b1b9-670e9e1abfbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371564} {:answer/id #uuid "60f3e6a6-8655-5b60-9a70-a8587f1955b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371565} {:answer/id #uuid "f0af26a0-a817-5ac8-9d1d-6b11d6565dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371566} {:answer/id #uuid "80711d17-2848-5c34-b142-eb70572e0d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0af26a0-a817-5ac8-9d1d-6b11d6565dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371566} :answer-field/id #uuid "d770571b-46f2-54a2-aa6f-7425ee59f7fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5225901d-ae4a-56db-a3e1-bd179cec1587" :question/math-academy-id "q-97960" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C-I$ is a singular matrix\n- $\\text{rank}(C-I)\\ne 2$\n- $\\text{nullity}(C-I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-I)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea2a7a2-d9fb-5c2b-b936-17cf236576e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371570} {:answer/id #uuid "23a8e9ff-b2b1-5d14-b67d-4b039c444461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371571} {:answer/id #uuid "425e462a-9222-5f1b-bf0c-708a8337ec08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371572} {:answer/id #uuid "8f267ff6-c3b6-5655-8242-e2fa8c874a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371573} {:answer/id #uuid "a61ffbae-ecdf-580f-846d-6296bc84e215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "425e462a-9222-5f1b-bf0c-708a8337ec08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371572} :answer-field/id #uuid "df675f47-c548-58d2-a400-5654b845da98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e96e49f-d26f-5fc8-b00b-d17cc89b1991" :question/math-academy-id "q-97962" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}$\n- $A-3I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A-3I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A-3I) \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is non-singular, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a98437ed-f615-5700-b247-0d4964210a9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371577} {:answer/id #uuid "a442745e-15b7-5d80-9c61-064b8496433c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371578} {:answer/id #uuid "595c2f55-b43f-5119-9d3b-4715388dffab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371579} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a9bfb6-0768-5894-ba9e-698570b02d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371580} {:answer/id #uuid "139b8b48-8ef7-53f1-9cb2-b78a05ea7374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "139b8b48-8ef7-53f1-9cb2-b78a05ea7374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371581} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5c3ea38-abea-58c3-ad4e-22aa6fad8088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "18416d37-8bc9-56ec-bba3-b0fbc5f74fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-97970" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(B-I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Col}(B-I)={R}^{3}$\n- $B-I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =1$ is not an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(B-I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(B-I) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(B-I)\\ne 3,$ according to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(B-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B-I$ is singular, then $(B-I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53589411-7e05-5ed6-8a4f-2c38ea39c75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371584} {:answer/id #uuid "5eabf63b-611e-5f51-a5ef-bfda9eb5a702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371585} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1db07f-2c8f-595e-b3b7-1abdaac0afde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371586} {:answer/id #uuid "11837017-a3a4-5ab6-a780-77728e17b598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371587} {:answer/id #uuid "02123059-258a-57f1-85fd-e09c283e168d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11837017-a3a4-5ab6-a780-77728e17b598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371587} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c7d216d-4421-51c1-943f-2412252a6f9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5cf1c55f-4902-57a2-b580-e6f6ee545445" :question/math-academy-id "q-97972" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Col}(C-2I)={R}^{2}$\n- $C-2I$ is non-singular\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(C-2I) & =2-\\text{nullity}(C-2I) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C-2I$ is singular, then $(C-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb57f54d-3d0f-5e5a-8367-217cd86e939e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371591} {:answer/id #uuid "61534bda-90c3-5674-bc41-ba018a3cfecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371592} {:answer/id #uuid "d644d173-15e6-5bcf-ab03-3ecd9f17cc0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371593} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca2946a-39c2-50d9-b79d-9738c3161e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371594} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1dce01-86f1-5135-b712-108acd05bdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb57f54d-3d0f-5e5a-8367-217cd86e939e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371591} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6ae590-ed86-508c-9580-ee7ab020421e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72db6877-b7dd-5ce6-a5f7-a207ef1aa8e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-97975" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(C+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Null}(C+3I)={0}$\n- $C+3I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C+3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(C+3I) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C+3I)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C+3I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C+3I$ is singular, then $(C+3I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
